WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:12.288
The next IP address topic we will cover is that of a dual IP stack. Simply put, a dual IP stack is the implementation of both IPv4 and IPv6 within an operating system.

00:12.288 --> 00:27.455
Common to a dual stack is an IPv4 mapped address, which is an IPv6 address with IPv4 numbering at the end. As an example, take the IPv4 address of 192.168.1.1.

00:27.455 --> 00:49.301
The IPv6 version that is mapped to this IPv4 address would be ::ffff:192.168.1.1. The :: represents zeros for the first 80 bits of a 128-bit IPv6 address.

00:49.301 --> 00:58.108
The ffff represent a 1 for the next 16 bits. And then the IPv4 address represents the last 32 bits.

00:58.108 --> 01:08.303
This exam topic on dual IP stacks is mentioned in both the Understand IPv4 and Understand IPv6 sections.

01:08.303 --> 01:14.618
Though we are covering it here in the Understand IPv4 section, it would behoove you to come back and view this again

01:14.618 --> 01:25.335
after you have gone through the first few topics in the IPv6 section, so you get both the IPv4 and IPv6 perspectives of a dual stack.

